(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does lung cancer metastasize to the brain? What is the vascular explanation?","choices":["Cells from the lung travel retrogradely up the inferior vena cava","Cells from the lung follow lymphatic drainage into the subarachnoid space","Cells enter the left heart and leave by the carotids","Cells from the lung enter the portal vein, which carries them to the brain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lung sits downstream of the pulmonary circulation, so its cells reach the systemic arterial side and the internal carotid takes them to the brain. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 53:38"},{"q":"Why can a neutrophil kill only a limited number of pathogens before dying?","choices":["It converts into a macrophage after a single encounter","It cannot renew its lysosomes, so it dies after phagocytosing a few pathogens","It lacks the enzymes needed to digest the bacterial cell wall it engulfs","It must first be opsonised before it can engulf anything"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The inability to renew lysosomes is precisely why the neutrophil is a short-lived, single-use phagocyte. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is inflammation?","choices":["The death of tissue through coagulative or liquefactive pathways","The reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury","The proliferation of fibroblasts following any cellular insult","The activation of the adaptive immune system by a foreign antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The definition given is specifically the reaction of vascularized living tissue to injury — vascularity is part of the definition, which is why avascular tissue cannot mount one. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What gives the spinous layer its alternative name of prickle cell layer?","choices":["Dead flattened keratinocytes packed into scales","Keratinocytes connected by intercellular connections called desmosomes, which form a spiny appearance","Keratohyalin granules that give the cells a rough and irregular outline when viewed on microscopy","Melanin granules transferred from melanocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This layer also contains the dendritic and Langerhans cells that act as immune sentinels. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Macular degeneration is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in which group?","choices":["Adults over seventy-five in the United States","Adults aged twenty to seventy-four in the United States","Children and adolescents","Adults of any age worldwide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the epidemiology. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the functional consequence of presbyopia?","choices":["Loss of peripheral vision","Loss of colour discrimination","Inability to focus on near objects, so reading glasses or bifocals are needed","Inability to focus on distant objects, so distance vision blurs first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and that is the everyday presentation. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which gene mutation is named for squamous cell carcinoma, and which histological features are pathognomonic?","choices":["Autosomal dominant inheritance with variable penetrance and radial growth","Mutation in the tp53 tumour suppressor gene, with keratin pearls and epithelial pearls as pathognomonic features","Overactivation of the Hedgehog pathway, with pearly papules and telangiectasias","Activating mutation of fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, with a stuck-on waxy and scaly appearance on the skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It derives from keratinocytes, and immunosuppression is a notable risk factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Henoch-Schonlein purpura involves which systems, and which patients?","choices":["The skin alone, with no systemic involvement whatsoever, at any age at all","Skin, joints, kidneys and intestines, commonly in children aged 3 to 10 years","Skin and lungs only, commonly in older adults","Skin and liver, commonly in neonates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an immunoglobulin A vasculitis, which is why it is not confined to the skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Basophils are chiefly responsible for which response, and through which mediator?","choices":["Antibody production, through immunoglobulin G","Cell-mediated immunity, through T cell receptors","Allergic and antigen response, through histamine","Parasitic defence, through major basic protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Basophils drive allergic and antigen response via histamine, which causes inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which history most separates labyrinthitis from Meniere disease?","choices":["A recent upper respiratory infection","The presence of sensorineural hearing loss","Unilateral rather than bilateral symptoms","The presence of tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis usually follows a viral infection, so a cold last week points hard at it; someone who has not had a cold in years is likelier to have Meniere disease. Both can be unilateral, so laterality does not separate them. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What happens to proliferation once a cell has committed to differentiation?","choices":["It stops entirely, and the differentiated cells become immortal","It stays at the same relatively low rate as the stem cell","It becomes autonomous and no longer requires physiologic stimuli","It can be dramatic, but those cells have a limited life-span"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the stem cell itself proliferates slowly; the committed progeny do the expansion, and then die. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"By what mechanism does diabetes mellitus cause cataract?","choices":["Excess glucose glycates the lens capsule until it ruptures","Hyperglycaemia causes crystallin proteins to aggregate directly","Retinal ischaemia releases vascular endothelial growth factor into the lens","Excess glucose is converted to sorbitol, causing osmotic swelling of the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the metabolic mechanism. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How differentiated are malignant tumours?","choices":["Always anaplastic","Always well differentiated","Various degrees of differentiation","Always poorly differentiated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — malignancy does not mean uniformly poor differentiation. Benign tumours are usually well differentiated. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Webster tied presbyopia to a letter in an examination mnemonic. Which, and what does it stand for?","choices":["The R in PERRLA, for reactive","The P in PERRLA, for pupils","The E in PERRLA, for equal","The A in PERRLA, for accommodation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pupils equal, round, reactive to light and accommodation. He quizzed the room on it. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"How are acute and chronic rhinosinusitis defined by duration, and what commonly accompanies the chronic form?","choices":["Acute under 2 weeks; chronic beyond 6 weeks, often with septal deviation","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks despite therapy, often with nasal polyps","Acute under 12 weeks; chronic beyond 6 months, often with turbinate hypertrophy","Acute under 4 weeks; chronic beyond 12 weeks, usually with no mucosal change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The two thresholds are the definition. Acute disease is usually viral &mdash; rhinovirus or influenza &mdash; with secondary bacterial infection by Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Exudation refers to the movement of what, and to where?","choices":["Fibrin into the vascular lumen to form a thrombus","Leukocytes from the interstitium back into the capillary lumen","Erythrocytes into the alveolar space","Fluid and blood proteins into the interstitial spaces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exudation follows the increased capillary permeability produced by endothelial contraction. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial otitis media?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans","Group A Streptococcus alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classic middle ear trio. Viral causes are respiratory syncytial virus, rhinovirus, influenza and adenovirus. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How do cisplatin and carboplatin cause permanent bilateral sensorineural loss?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in stria vascularis cells, compromising endolymph ion homeostasis","They fix the stapes footplate in the oval window","They generate reactive oxygen species that destroy outer hair cells at the cochlear base","They inhibit prestin in outer hair cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stria vascularis maintains the ionic composition of endolymph, and without that gradient transduction fails. Damage there is permanent and bilateral. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A patient has thick, leathery plaques on the neck and forearms with no scaling, after months of scratching. Which secondary lesion is this, mechanistically?","choices":["Ulcer, arising from focal loss of both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it","Atrophy, from slowed keratinocyte division","Lichenification, from hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis driven by the itch-scratch cycle","Scale, from desquamating stratum corneum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of scaling is what separates it from psoriasis, whose plaques do scale. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which cells are the agranulocytes (mononuclear leucocytes)?","choices":["Basophils, mast cells and macrophages","Lymphocytes (B cells and T cells), monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils","Erythrocytes, platelets and circulating plasma cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These three are grouped as the agranulocytes or mononuclear leucocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is known about the pathogenesis of macular degeneration overall?","choices":["It is a consequence of raised intraocular pressure","It is unknown","It is entirely genetic","It is entirely driven by ultraviolet exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pathogenesis is unknown for both forms. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why does a deviated septum cause epistaxis and loss of smell?","choices":["Compression of the ethmoid arteries and the olfactory nerve","Turbulent airflow dries the mucosa; air misses the cribriform plate","Chronic infection eroding the septal vessels and olfactory epithelium","Autoimmune destruction of the septal cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two separate consequences of the same disturbed airflow: local drying with fragile vessel breakdown on one hand, and failure to deliver odorant to the superior nasal vault on the other. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What happens to the wider nasal cavity opposite a septal deviation?","choices":["It atrophies from disuse","It develops polyps from the increased flow","Nothing changes; only the narrow side is affected","Compensatory inferior turbinate hypertrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The wide side does more of the work, so its turbinate enlarges to condition the extra airflow &mdash; which can then narrow the only good airway the patient had. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Middle ear effusion and cholesteatoma impair conduction by which mechanism?","choices":["Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity","Obstruction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both add fluid or tissue weight that damps movement of the tympanic membrane and ossicles. The chain is intact and mobile in principle, but it is carrying extra mass. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of a hypopigmented macule such as vitiligo, against a hyperpigmented one?","choices":["Loss of melanocytes, against increased melanin production in the basal layer of the epidermis","Increased melanin production within the epidermis, as against loss of melanocytes entirely","Capillary rupture with blood deposition in the dermis","Dilatation and thinning of superficial dermal vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ephelides, café au lait spots and Mongolian spots are the hyperpigmented examples given. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which human papillomavirus subtypes most commonly cause cervical cancer?","choices":["Types 1 and 2","Types 31 and 33","Types 16 and 18","Types 6 and 11"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — of many genetic subtypes, these two cause the majority. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Haemorrhagic inflammation is described as arising from what, and occurring in what relationship to other exudates?","choices":["Epithelial sloughing, and replacing other exudates","Damage to blood vessels, and occurring together with other exudates","Mucus hypersecretion, and occurring in isolation","Fibrinogen deposition, and preceding every other exudate pattern"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Haemorrhagic inflammation is damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which structure separates the external ear from the middle ear, and what does it do?","choices":["The oval window, which passes energy to the cochlea","The round window, which relieves pressure","The Eustachian tube, which equalises pressure","The tympanic membrane, which vibrates and passes energy to the ossicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is the boundary and the first moving part of the conducting chain, which is why perforating it causes conductive loss. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which vascular event immediately follows increased vascular permeability in the reviewed sequence?","choices":["Haemoconcentration and stasis","Chemotaxis","Phagocytosis","Vasoconstriction of the arterioles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As fluid and protein leave the vessel, the remaining blood concentrates and flow slows — which is what allows leukocytes to marginate and adhere next. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why are genes promoting self destruction of cells with damaged deoxyribonucleic acid necessary?","choices":["To limit the total number of cells an organ can contain","To supply nutrients to neighbouring cells as they proliferate","To slow the rate at which stem cells commit to differentiation","To stop the damage becoming permanent in dividing cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — apoptosis prevents a mutation being carried on into the daughter cells. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the growth phases of melanoma, in order?","choices":["Sensitization then elicitation","Radial growth within the epidermis, then vertical growth penetrating the dermis, then increasing tumour thickness, then metastatic invasion of lymphatics and blood vessels","Vertical growth down into the dermis first, then radial spread within the epidermis afterwards once the tumour has thickened sufficiently to invade","Inflammatory, proliferative, then remodelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The shift from radial to vertical is the point at which depth begins to matter. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the primary component of the dermis, which cell produces it, and what does it provide?","choices":["Melanin, produced by melanocytes, providing ultraviolet protection","Collagen, produced by fibroblasts, providing tensile strength","Keratin, produced by keratinocytes, providing an impermeable barrier","Elastin, produced by mast cells, providing tensile strength"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with elastic fibres of elastin and fibrillin providing elastic recoil and flexibility alongside it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two structures make up the fibrous outer tunic of the eye?","choices":["The sclera and the cornea","The choroid and the ciliary body","The retina and the retinal pigment epithelium","The iris and the lens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the protective white coat and the clear refracting window. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Cerumen impaction, foreign bodies and canal exostoses all cause conductive loss by which of the four core mechanisms?","choices":["Obstruction","Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A physical blockage stops sound penetrating down the canal. It is the first of four mechanisms, and the only one that acts before the sound reaches the drum. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the classical corneal finding in herpes simplex epithelial keratitis?","choices":["A geographic ulcer with a hypopyon","A ring infiltrate in a contact lens wearer","A wedge of fibrovascular tissue growing across and over the corneal limbus","A dendritic ulcer with terminal bulbs on fluorescein staining"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — terminal bulbs included. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A nodule is greater than 5 millimetres. What tissue does it arise from?","choices":["Fluid accumulating between epidermis and dermis","Dilated superficial dermal capillaries with no inflammatory infiltration at all","Dermal-based tissue with dense collagen bundles, extending into subcutaneous tissue","Superficial epidermis only, with no dermal involvement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epidermoid cysts are keratin-filled, dermatofibroma is fibrous tissue, and a rheumatoid nodule is fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by histiocytes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which findings does orbital cellulitis show that periorbital cellulitis lacks?","choices":["Erythema and oedema of the lid alone, held forward by the orbital septum","Crusting of the lid margin with altered tear film","A yellowish nodule that does not cross the cornea","Proptosis, decreased extraocular movement and a risk of optic nerve compression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all consequences of a post-septal collection. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of primary open-angle glaucoma?","choices":["The iridocorneal angle stays open but microscopic resistance in the trabecular meshwork impairs outflow, raising pressure gradually","The iridocorneal angle narrows anatomically and blocks aqueous outflow suddenly","Aqueous production by the ciliary body increases sharply","The canal of Schlemm is congenitally absent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the angle is open; the obstruction is microscopic and downstream. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of urticaria?","choices":["Capillary rupture with extravasation of blood","Mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, producing wheals","T cell-mediated delayed hypersensitivity 48 to 72 hours after exposure","Dysplastic keratinocyte proliferation from ultraviolet damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it may be acute or chronic with a wide range of triggers. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Where do melanocytes normally sit, and what drives their malignant transformation?","choices":["At the dermal-epidermal junction, with ultraviolet light and oxidative stress damaging melanocyte DNA and leading to uninhibited growth","Within the stratum corneum, transformed by keratinase digestion","In the reticular layer of the dermis, transformed by dysregulation of the fibroblasts that reside within it over a long period of time","In the basal layer, transformed by Hedgehog pathway activation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the risk is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait with variable penetrance. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the tissue distinction between an erosion and an ulcer?","choices":["An erosion is focal loss of epidermis that does not penetrate below the dermal-epidermal junction; an ulcer is focal loss of epidermis AND dermis, with destruction of collagen and infiltration of inflammatory cells","An erosion involves both the epidermis and the dermis beneath it; an ulcer is limited to the epidermis alone and spares the dermal-epidermal junction entirely, so it never destroys collagen at all","An erosion is fluid-filled and an ulcer is solid","An erosion is a primary lesion and an ulcer is a secondary lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The junction is the boundary that separates them, and crossing it is what brings collagen destruction into play. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of labyrinthitis, and why does it impair hearing AND balance together?","choices":["Dislodged otoconia striking structures in the endolymph","Fixation of the stapes footplate","Inflammation across both the semicircular canals and the cochlea","Defective endolymph resorption ballooning the scala media"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The inflammation does not respect the boundary between the vestibular apparatus and the organ of Corti, so both fail at once. That simultaneous involvement is what names it otitis interna. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What determines where a cancer metastasizes?","choices":["Venous blood flow, tumour and endothelial receptors, and genetic fitness","Chance alone, since circulating tumour cells lodge wherever the vessel first narrows","The size of the primary lesion and the duration of the disease","The histological grade of the tumour and its rate of mitosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — metastatic spread is NOT random but determined by these three. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why does a tumour mass grow faster than normal tissue?","choices":["A lower proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","An unchanged proliferative fraction with a shorter individual cell cycle","A higher proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","A higher proliferative fraction together with a higher rate of cell loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both halves matter; it is not only that cells divide faster. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which four histological features move a tumour from the benign end towards the malignant end?","choices":["Encapsulation, uniform nuclei, absent mitoses, and normal differentiation","Invasion, lymphatic spread, haematogenous spread, and seeding","Initiation, promotion, progression, and metastasis","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, and abnormal differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — these lie on a spectrum rather than a switch. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What causes astigmatism?","choices":["An axial globe that is too short","Loss of lens elasticity with age, so the lens can no longer change shape","Irregular corneal or lens curvature creating non-spherical focal points","An axial globe that is too long"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why a single focal point cannot form. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which two mediators are plasma proteins?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G and C3b","Fibrinogen and fibrolytic enzymes","Bradykinin and complement components","Histamine and serotonin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed under the plasma proteins heading, alongside the coagulation system. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which two of the listed subsets both act against virus-infected and tumour cells?","choices":["B lymphocytes and plasma cells","CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells","Monocytes and macrophages","T helper cells and regulatory suppressor T cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both target virus-infected and tumour cells. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are vocal cord nodules, and what causes them?","choices":["Unilateral pedunculated fluid-filled growths on the middle third, from a single severe strain","Bilateral wart-like exophytic lesions from viral infection","Unilateral firm masses with cord fixation, from malignancy","Bilateral symmetrical calluses at the anterior third junction, from chronic phonotrauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Repeated slamming of the cords causes basement membrane hyalinisation &mdash; literally a callus. Bilateral and symmetrical, because both cords take the same repeated impact at the same point. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which forms of radiation cause cancer?","choices":["Ultraviolet B, and ionizing radiation","Ultraviolet A only, together with infrared radiation","Microwave and radiofrequency radiation","Ionizing radiation only, with no ultraviolet contribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ultraviolet B is the wavelength that damages deoxyribonucleic acid directly. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Is the vertigo of labyrinthitis episodic or continuous, and how long does it run?","choices":["Episodic, with attacks lasting several hours","Episodic, with attacks lasting under a minute","Continuous, permanent and non-improving","Continuous, over days, improving across weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Someone comes in saying the room has been spinning for three days, not that it comes and goes. Continuous vertigo that slowly settles over weeks is the labyrinthitis pattern, because the body is resolving an inflammatory process. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17 (slide says episodic; corrected in lecture)"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is described as the first responder to microbial infection?","choices":["The eosinophil","The B lymphocyte","The neutrophil","The macrophage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil is named as the first responder to microbial infection, defending against bacterial and fungal infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which three cell types are granulocytes?","choices":["Lymphocytes, monocytes and macrophages","Neutrophils, basophils and eosinophils","Neutrophils, monocytes and B lymphocytes","Basophils, eosinophils and natural killer cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the three granulocytes; polymorphonuclear leukocyte refers to all granulocytes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What forms during the proliferative phase, and what is it made of?","choices":["A fibrin haemostatic plug, over days 1 to 3","Type I collagen laid down in parallel bundles, over days 21 to one year of remodelling","Keratohyalin granules within the granular layer","Granulation tissue, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells, over days 4 to 21"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those three cell types supply clearance, matrix and new vessels respectively. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What are the symptoms of rhegmatogenous detachment?","choices":["Gradual painless bilateral blurring with glare at night","Severe pain with halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil","Slow loss of central detail with progressive distortion of straight lines","Flashing lights, a shower of floaters, and a curtain falling across the field"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — photopsia, floaters, then the curtain. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why does skeletal muscle hypertrophy rather than divide?","choices":["Protein intake causes fluid retention within the muscle, so the organ appears larger","Resistance training converts slow fibres to fast fibres, changing the tissue type","Striated muscle cells do not divide, so each fibre gets larger instead","Resistance training recruits satellite cells, so the number of muscle fibres increases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — protein and resistance build more actin and myosin within the existing cells. Bigger fibres, not more fibres, which is the whole distinction from hyperplasia. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 2:54"},{"q":"What happens when a protooncogene mutates?","choices":["It becomes a repair gene, which then introduces errors into the genome","It becomes silent, so the cell loses its ability to grow at all","It becomes an oncogene, which encodes oncoproteins that promote continued uncontrolled growth","It becomes a tumour suppressor gene, which then inhibits cell growth excessively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same machinery, with the brake removed. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which two abnormalities separate a tumour cell from a normal cell?","choices":["Abnormal apoptosis, and abnormal deoxyribonucleic acid repair","Abnormal vascular supply, and abnormal stromal composition","Abnormal differentiation, and abnormal cell size","Abnormal regulation of cell growth, and abnormal cell-cell interactions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same slide also contrasts a normal stem cell with a cancer stem cell. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What causes an ipsilateral optic nerve lesion?","choices":["Posterior cerebral artery occlusion in the occipital lobe","Temporal lobe surgery","Trauma, optic neuritis and ischaemic optic neuropathy","Pituitary adenoma compression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the three causes at site A. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Where do T lymphocytes mature, and what surface structure do they carry?","choices":["The thymus; T cell receptors","The bone marrow; Fc receptors","The spleen; complement receptors","The lymph nodes; major histocompatibility complexes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — matures in the Thymus, hence T lymphocyte, and it carries the T cell receptor. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
